WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.229 --> 00:00:04.890
Dear Fellow Scholars, this is Two Minute Papers
with Dr. Károly Zsolnai-Fehér.

00:00:04.890 --> 00:00:10.440
Today we are going to take a bunch of completely
unorganized human motion data, give it to

00:00:10.440 --> 00:00:17.620
an AI, grab a controller, and get an amazing
video game out of it, which we can play almost

00:00:17.620 --> 00:00:19.000
immediately.

00:00:19.000 --> 00:00:20.279
Why almost immediately?

00:00:20.279 --> 00:00:22.769
I’ll tell you in a moment.

00:00:22.769 --> 00:00:25.000
So how does this process work?

00:00:25.000 --> 00:00:30.349
There are similar previous techniques that
took a big soup of motion capture data and

00:00:30.349 --> 00:00:34.270
outbid each other what they could learn from
it.

00:00:34.270 --> 00:00:40.000
And they did it really well - for instance,
one these AIs was able to not only learn these

00:00:40.000 --> 00:00:46.829
movements, but even improve them, and even
better, adapt them to different kinds of terrains.

00:00:46.829 --> 00:00:52.670
This other work used a small training set
of general movements to reinvent a popular

00:00:52.670 --> 00:00:56.510
high jump technique, the Fosbury flop by itself.

00:00:56.510 --> 00:01:03.010
This allows the athlete to jump backward over
the bar, thus lowering their center of gravity.

00:01:03.010 --> 00:01:08.010
And, it could also do it on Mars.

00:01:08.010 --> 00:01:09.160
So cool!

00:01:09.160 --> 00:01:15.160
In the meantime, Ubisoft also learned to not
only simulate, but even predict the future

00:01:15.160 --> 00:01:19.220
motion of video game characters, thus speeding
up this process.

00:01:19.220 --> 00:01:26.670
You can see here how well its predictions
line up with the real reference footage.

00:01:26.670 --> 00:01:30.780
And it could also stand its ground when previous
methods fail.

00:01:30.780 --> 00:01:31.950
Ouch.

00:01:31.950 --> 00:01:34.380
So, are we done here?

00:01:34.380 --> 00:01:36.670
Is there nothing else to do?

00:01:36.670 --> 00:01:38.570
Of course there is!

00:01:38.570 --> 00:01:44.290
Here, in goes this soup of unorganized motion
data, which is given to train a deep neural

00:01:44.290 --> 00:01:47.040
network, and then…this happens!

00:01:47.040 --> 00:01:53.310
Yes, the AI learned how to weave together
these motions so well that we can even grab

00:01:53.310 --> 00:01:56.600
a controller, and start playing immediately.

00:01:56.600 --> 00:01:59.480
Or…almost immediately.

00:01:59.480 --> 00:02:01.490
And with that, here comes the problem.

00:02:01.490 --> 00:02:03.200
Do you see it?

00:02:03.200 --> 00:02:09.069
There is still a bit of a delay between the
button press and the motion.

00:02:09.069 --> 00:02:14.730
A simple way of alleviating that would be
to speed up the underlying animations.

00:02:14.730 --> 00:02:21.049
Well, that’s not going to be it, because
this way, the motions lose their realism.

00:02:21.049 --> 00:02:22.049
Not good.

00:02:22.049 --> 00:02:24.640
So, can we do something about this?

00:02:24.640 --> 00:02:30.609
Well, hold on to your papers, and have a look
at how the new method addresses it!

00:02:30.609 --> 00:02:37.489
We press the button, and, yes, now that is
what I call quick, and fluid motion.

00:02:37.489 --> 00:02:43.909
Yes, this new AI promises to learn time-critical
responses to our commands.

00:02:43.909 --> 00:02:45.079
What does that mean?

00:02:45.079 --> 00:02:46.830
Well, see the white bar here?

00:02:46.830 --> 00:02:53.069
By this amount of time, the motions have to
finish, and the blue is where we are currently.

00:02:53.069 --> 00:02:58.879
So the time critical part means that it promises
that the blue bar will never exceed the white

00:02:58.879 --> 00:03:00.219
bar.

00:03:00.219 --> 00:03:05.599
This is wonderful, just ask a gamer this:
has it happened that you already pressed the

00:03:05.599 --> 00:03:09.590
button, but the action didn’t execute in
time.

00:03:09.590 --> 00:03:12.950
They will say that it happens all the time.

00:03:12.950 --> 00:03:19.579
But with this, we can perform a series of
slow U-turns, and then, progressively decrease

00:03:19.579 --> 00:03:25.610
the amount of time that we give to the character,
and see how much more agile it becomes.

00:03:25.610 --> 00:03:27.090
Absolutely amazing.

00:03:27.090 --> 00:03:31.849
The motions really change, and I find all
of them quite realistic.

00:03:31.849 --> 00:03:37.290
Maybe this could be connected to a game mechanic
where the super quick, time critical actions

00:03:37.290 --> 00:03:43.290
deplete the stamina of our character quicker,
so we have to use them sparingly.

00:03:43.290 --> 00:03:45.530
But that’s not all it can do.

00:03:45.530 --> 00:03:46.530
Not even close.

00:03:46.530 --> 00:03:52.500
We can even chain many of these crazy actions
together, and as you see, our character does

00:03:52.500 --> 00:03:58.010
these amazing motions that looks like it came
straight out of The Witcher series.

00:03:58.010 --> 00:03:59.269
Loving it.

00:03:59.269 --> 00:04:04.660
What you see here it a teacher network that
learns to efficiently pull off these moves,

00:04:04.660 --> 00:04:10.540
and then, we fire up a student neural network
that seeks to become as proficient as the

00:04:10.540 --> 00:04:15.129
teacher, but with a smaller and more compact
neural network.

00:04:15.129 --> 00:04:17.870
This is what we call policy distillation.

00:04:17.870 --> 00:04:22.620
So, is the student as good as its teacher?

00:04:22.620 --> 00:04:27.980
Let’s have one more look at the teacher…and
the student.

00:04:27.980 --> 00:04:29.540
They are very close.

00:04:29.540 --> 00:04:33.250
Actually, wait a second…did you see it?

00:04:33.250 --> 00:04:37.820
The student is actually even more responsive
than its teacher was.

00:04:37.820 --> 00:04:40.620
This example showcases it more clearly.

00:04:40.620 --> 00:04:48.000
It can even complete this slalom course, and
we might even make a parkour game with it.

00:04:48.000 --> 00:04:49.660
And here comes the best part.

00:04:49.660 --> 00:04:56.950
The required training data is not a few days,
not even a few hours, only a few minutes,

00:04:56.950 --> 00:05:03.020
and the training time is in the order of a
few hours, and this we only have to do once,

00:05:03.020 --> 00:05:07.950
and we are free to use the trained neural
network for as long as we please.

00:05:07.950 --> 00:05:14.380
Now, one more really cool tidbit in this work
is that this training data mustn’t be realistic,

00:05:14.380 --> 00:05:21.480
it can be a soup of highly stylized motions,
and it can still weave them together really

00:05:21.480 --> 00:05:22.480
well.

00:05:22.480 --> 00:05:25.200
It is possible that?

00:05:25.200 --> 00:05:28.690
Yes, my goodness, it is possible!

00:05:28.690 --> 00:05:33.680
The input motions don’t even have to come
from humans, they can come from quadrupeds

00:05:33.680 --> 00:05:34.680
too.

00:05:34.680 --> 00:05:40.760
This took only 5 minutes of motion capture
data, and the horse AI became able to transition

00:05:40.760 --> 00:05:43.110
between these movement types.

00:05:43.110 --> 00:05:48.140
This is yet another amazing tool in democratizing
character animation.

00:05:48.140 --> 00:05:50.210
Absolutely amazing.

00:05:50.210 --> 00:06:18.650
What a 

00:06:18.650 --> 00:06:29.300
time 

00:06:29.300 --> 00:06:38.120
to be alive!

00:06:38.120 --> 00:06:48.440
Thanks for watching and for your generous
support, and I'll see you next time!

